public void PUT_IndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList)
{
    int x = PexSelect.First<int>();
    // you could change this to a foreach based on what suits you better
    // foreach (Type t in PexSelect.All<Type>()) {...}

    // the rest of your testing code here
}
```

Note: You need to specify multiple x's (integers) for testing.

Here I'm using PexSelect instead of PexAssume since PexSelect allows you to select from all possible values to test your precondition. PexAssume allows you to specify a subset of values to test. Both have their place and often can be used together.

This should give you a good basic understanding of how you can create contracts about data structures in your code.

You may also want to read some more on formal specification, behavioral correctness and contract testing, these can provide more background or insights on how to go about this.

Also keep in mind that ArrayList.IndexOf has a worst-case complexity of O(n), which makes it potentially inefficient for large lists. For linear time bounds operation, consider using different data structures like HashSet or SortedSet.

A full understanding of contract testing can be quite complex and might require knowledge in several areas of computer science including formal specification, behavioral correctness and theorem proving or model checking. It is a field with its own subject to study for more details, but here are some resources that can provide more clarity:

1. Understanding Formal Specification: https://www.cs.utexas.edu/users/mckinley/380s2001/papers/plp00.pdf

2. Behaviour Driven Development: https://bradfrost.github.io/this-is-bacon/

3. Learning about theorem proving or model checking: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Model_checking

4. Tools to help with contract testing: https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/project/contractchk/

5. Other articles and courses: https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220339703_Correct_by_Construction_Software_Testing

Remember, good unit-tests are not enough, you should include some good contract tests too. They can prevent many more bugs by showing that the data structure and algorithms are working correctly without having to explore all the different ways a data structure could fail.

Good luck.
