Q: How to pass values to my ViewModel in Android? I know that there are a lot of topics with this subject, but I couldn't find a detailed explanation that could cover everything.
So, I have an Android app which is using Model-View-ViewModel (MVVM) architecture.
I would like to pass some values from the Repository to the ViewModel. 
Let's take an example:


*

*Activity (UI) makes a call to Repository (Data Layer) to retrieve some data.

*Activity passes some values to Repository (e.g., userId)

*Repository retrieves the data based on the values passed.

*Repository then passes back the data to the ViewModel.

*Finally, the ViewModel posts the data to an Observable field that the Activity observes.


Now, I see that this is quite common, but how do I implement it?
I'm sorry if this is too basic - this is just starting to get to understand MVVM and I might miss something important here.
Here's what I have so far:
Activity:
ViewModel = ViewModelProvider(this).get(ViewModel::class.java)
ViewModel.fetchUserData(userId)
observeUserData()

fun fetchUserData(userId: String) {
    val data = repository.getUserData(userId)
    viewModelScope.launch {
        userData.postValue(data)
    }
}

ViewModel:
private val _userData = MutableLiveData<User>()
val userData: LiveData<User> get() = _userData

Repository:
fun getUserData(userId: String): User {
    // Here I would pass userId to my API

    val response = service.getUser(userId)
    return response.body()
}

So far, everything works as expected. My question is, how should I modify my ViewModel to pass userId from the Activity to the Repository?
Thank you.

A: You should move the userId parameter to your fetchUserData function within the ViewModel instead of being a parameter of your Activity, because the ViewModel has the responsibility to store and manage the UI related data.
Here is a little adjusted code:
ViewModel:
fun fetchUserData(userId: String) {
    viewModelScope.launch {
        val data = repository.getUserData(userId)
        _userData.postValue(data)
    }
}

Activity:
viewModel.fetchUserData(userId)
observeUserData()

...

private fun observeUserData() {
    viewModel.userData.observe(this, Observer { user ->
        // Update the UI here based on the user data
    })
}

In your Repository, where you use viewModelScope.launch {...} then the work you are doing inside that block will be automatically managed by the lifecycle of your ViewModel. That is why you don't need to cancel the request when your activity is destroyed. It will be cancelled automatically.

A: You should use parameters and functions inside your ViewModel as much as possible. This way, you will prevent communication from your Repository to your Activity. 
