The arraylist is created with some element, and the method returns the index of a given value in collection. 

If the value is not in collection, the method returns -1. So it doesn't require that the value should be in the arraylist before using that method.

And it's expected that its count won't change and the 'Contains' method will return true if the value is still in collection after the method call.

It should also satisfy the property of being the last seen index in List or ArrayList before it's removed or modified. So the LastIndexOf should be smaller or equal to the Count after the operation. 

This is the initial method call and we can call it precondition, therefore I marked with PexAssumeUnderTest.

The method that is called in the test is List<T>.IndexOf(T) from .NET framework.

And we assume the List contains T element as per its declaration.

Also this is just a part of several test cases, PexFramework has more sophisticated features as well to test mutiple branches, edge cases, etc.

For more detailed info see NUnit, Pex, and Static Analysis – Microsoft Research, C# Programming Guide - NUnit.org. 

A: It may because the PexFramework is a mutation testing tool, and the Pex framework automatically tries to find inputs which make mutation, i.e., "killing" mutants in your code. The Mutants must be killed, meaning the code path that was being exercised must be removed/disabled as per the mutation point.

The Pex algorithm determines which mutations will kill more or maintain mutants, the more complex the mutation, the lower the chance it gets killed. For instance, changing an integer to another value or renaming an object are examples of simple mutations that get killed easily. The more advanced ones, like changing an operation or an expression with no side-effects won't get killed quite often.

In your case, it seems there is no mutation point to test the case you mentioned since this method do not have any side effect, but just return a value. All you can do to ensure it's okay is to check whether it's still working fine after the mutation. This implies that the method works perfectly fine, no matter the input so there can't be a mutation to be killed/killed. 

Moreover, the mutation testing tool usually focuses on the mutation points in methods with side-effects or methods with complex logic, because in those methods, there is still a chance for the mutant to be killed if the program does not behave as expected.

Hope this will help you to understand the concept of mutation and how it is applied to your specific case.
Ref: http://research.microsoft.com/en-us/um/cambridge/projects/automated-mutation-testing/industrial-case-study.aspx

A: The issue seems to be with test generation as the code works fine and it should not break. ArrayList has a Count property and the PexRunner is considering it a mutation site so it tries to test it and passes the assertion.
The Count property is read-only and does not have a setter so PexRunner considers it as a mutation site thus causing the issue.
Also, the framework is trying to find inputs that could generate an exception or a different result i.e., it should be working with a mutation.
To prevent this behavior in future tests consider following approaches:


*

*Avoid mutation sites by using PexMethod, PexAssembly etc.

*Don't mark mutation sites as test targets.

*Use attributes such as PexAllowedExceptionAttribute to specify which exceptions are allowed by your tests.

*In some edge cases you might need to add preconditions for the mutation site.


It seems your code is fine, however, there's no guarantee that it will be perfect with all future test runs. This is why it's so important to write valid tests and run the tests frequently to get the best coverage of your automated tests.
It's a known behavior in the Pex runner and Microsoft has plans to improve testing experience in future versions of Pex.
Please refer for more understanding: https://microsoft.github.io/testable/docs/nunit/pex/

A: It seems a bit counterintuitive, but in that method--------------------	    //   i.e., if the return value > 0 => old array last index of x <= new array last index of x
	    Assert.IsTrue(
	          Old_arrListLastIndexOfX < 0 || New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= 0 &&
	             New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);

            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@ If x is in old array, it will be in new array with a smaller index or same index
            //  i.e., if both arrays contain x and old array's last index of x >= new array's last index of x, assert it is true 
            Assert.IsTrue(
                Old_arrListContainsX && New_arrListContainsX &&
                New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@ If the return value is -1, no elements in the array equals to x
	    //  i.e., if ret is -1 then old arrList does not contain x, which implies arrList.Count == Old_arrListCount 
	    Assert.IsTrue(New_Ret == -1 ? Old_arrListContainsX == false : arrList.Count == Old_arrListCount+1);

	    // @@ When x already in the array, the return value should not increase
	    Assert.IsTrue(!Old_arrListContainsX || New_Ret >= Old_arrListCount ? New_Ret == Old_arrListCount : New_Ret == Old_arrListCount+1);
        }
    }
}

//ArrayList/Assumptions/ArrayListContractTest.cs
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework.Validation;
using ArrayListNamespace;
using System;

namespace ArrayList.Assumptions
{
    [PexClass(typeof(ArrayListNamespace.ArrayList))]
    [PexAllowedExceptionFromTypeUnderTest("System.ArgumentOutOfRangeException", PexAllowedExceptionFromType.Secondary)]
    [PexAllowedExceptionFromTypeUnderTest("System.IndexOutOfRangeException", PexAllowedExceptionFromType.Secondary)]
    [PexAllowedExceptionFromTypeUnderTest("System.NullReferenceException", PexAllowedExceptionFromType.Secondary)]
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        [PexMethod]
        [PexExploration]
        [PexPostcondition(MethodName = "Contains", Arguments = new object[] { "oldArg_arrList", "oldArg_item" })]
        public bool Add([PexObjectCreation("System.Collections.ArrayList")] ArrayList arrList, [PexAssumeNotNull] object item)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int OldCount = arrList.Count;

            bool New_Ret = arrList.Add(item);

            int NewCount = arrList.Count;

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$oldCount", OldCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$newRet", New_Ret);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$newCount", NewCount);

            Assert.AreEqual(OldCount+1, NewCount);
            Assert.IsTrue(New_Ret);
            AssumePostcondition.IsTrue(arrList.Contains(item));

            return New_Ret;
        }
    [PexMethod]
    [PexExploration]
    [PexPostcondition(MethodName = "Contains", Arguments = new object[] { "oldArg_arrList", "oldArg_item" })]
    public int Add([PexObjectCreation("System.Collections.ArrayList")] ArrayList arrList, [PexAssumeNotNull] object item, int index)
    {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int OldCount = arrList.Count;

            int NewRet = arrList.Add(item, index);

            int NewCount = arrList.Count;

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$oldCount", OldCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$newRet", NewRet);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$item", item);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$newCount", NewCount);

            Assert.AreEqual--------------------            Debug.Assert(
                Old_arrListContainsX
                && Old_arrListLastIndexOfX <= New_arrListLastIndexOfX
                && New_Ret >= 0,
                "The Last Index of x in an array has been increased or x is not present in target array.");

            // assert that if the return value is negative, no other condition is true
            Debug.Assert(
                New_Ret < 0
                && !(Old_arrListContainsX && Old_arrListLastIndexOfX <= New_arrListLastIndexOfX),
                "The return value should be negative if x is not present in target array.");
        }
    }
}﻿using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Text;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

namespace CaixaEletronico
{
    public class Caixa
    {
        private static int totalCaixa = 100000; // valor em centavos - total do dinheiro no caixa
        public static string Retirar(int valor)
        {
            if (valor > totalCaixa)
            {
                return "Saldo insuficiente!";
            }
            else
            {
                totalCaixa -= valor;
                return "Retirada realizada com sucesso!";
            }
        }
        public static string Depositar(int valor)
        {
            totalCaixa += valor;
            return "Depósito realizado com sucesso!";
        }
        public static string Saldo()
        {
            return "R$" + (totalCaixa / 100.0).ToString("0.00");
        }
    }
}﻿using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.IO;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

namespace Infrastructure.Utility
{
    public class LocalStorage : IStorage
    {
        public async Task<string> SaveAsync(byte[] bytes, string folderName, string fileName, string extension)
        {
            try
            {
                var documentsPath = Environment.GetFolderPath(Environment.SpecialFolder.Personal);
                var libPath = Path.Combine(documentsPath, folderName);

                if (!Directory.Exists(libPath))
                {
                    Directory.CreateDirectory(libPath);
                }

                var filePath = Path.Combine(libPath, fileName + extension);

                // ReSharper disable once AssignNullToNotNullAttribute
                using (var stream = new FileStream(filePath, FileMode.Create))
                {
                    await stream.WriteAsync(bytes, 0, bytes.Length);
                }

                return filePath;
            }
            catch (Exception e)
            {
                // TODO: add logging mechanism
                throw new ApplicationException("Error saving the file.", e);
            }
        }
    }
}﻿using System.Threading.Tasks;

namespace Infrastructure.Utility
{
    public interface IStorage
    {
        Task<string> SaveAsync(byte[] bytes, string folderName, string fileName, string extension);
    }
}//Infrastructure/Infrastructure/Utility/StorageFactory.cs
﻿namespace Infrastructure.Utility
{
    public class StorageFactory
    {
        public static IStorage Create(StorageType storageType)
        {
            switch (storageType)
            {
                case StorageType.Local:
                    return new LocalStorage();

                case StorageType.Cloud:
                    return new CloudStorage();

                default:
                    return new LocalStorage();
            }
        } 
    }
}

